---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { REPO } from "../lib/site";
// The privacy policy of the Mac app, the iPhone and Apple Watch apps, the browser
// extension (ADR 0021), and this site. The Chrome Web Store listing of the extension and
// the App Store listing of the iPhone app link here. Keep it true to the code:
// docs/operations/browser.md, docs/adr/0015-automatic-updates.md,
// docs/adr/0022-relay-sync.md, and docs/store/app-store/app-privacy.md (the App Privacy
// answers must say the same as this page).
const UPDATED = "2026-11-03";
---
<Page title="Privacy · apassy" description="What Apassy for the Mac, iPhone, and Apple Watch, its browser extension, its relay, and this site do with your data.">
  <main class="container">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Your secrets stay on your devices</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Apassy keeps your credentials on your Mac, your iPhone, and your Apple Watch. There are no Apassy accounts, no
        analytics, and no ads. Your vault, your agents' requests, and your answers leave your devices only encrypted, and
        we cannot read them. Updated {UPDATED}.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The Apassy app</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your vault is a file on your Mac, encrypted with your passphrase (SQLCipher). Apassy reads it only on this Mac.</li>
        <li>
          The vault leaves your Mac only when you choose it: a backup file that you save, or sync through a folder that you
          pick (iCloud Drive, Dropbox, and others) or through the Apassy relay. What goes there is the encrypted vault
          file. Nobody can read it without your passphrase.
        </li>
        <li>
          Apassy checks for updates: it reads <code>https://apassy.wyderka.cc/latest.json</code> after the start and then
          every 6 hours. Like any web request, it carries the IP address of your Mac. You can turn the check off in Settings.
        </li>
        <li>
          The bouncer brain runs on your Mac. It sends no agent command and none of your rules anywhere to decide. If you
          answer requests on your iPhone or Apple Watch, each request goes to them sealed, as described below.
        </li>
        <li>The iPhone and Apple Watch apps, and the Apassy relay that they use, are described in the next sections.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The iPhone and Apple Watch apps</h2>
      <p>
        The iPhone app opens the same encrypted vault as your Mac and fills passwords, one-time codes, and passkeys. The
        Apple Watch app answers the requests of your agents. Neither app has accounts, ads, analytics, crash reporting, or
        tracking.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your vault on the iPhone.</strong> The vault is an encrypted file on your iPhone (SQLCipher), opened
          with your passphrase. If you turn on Face ID unlock, the passphrase is kept in the iPhone's keychain on this
          device only. It never goes to iCloud Keychain or to us. Apassy asks for Face ID or your passphrase before it
          shows, copies, or fills a secret: each time, or after the time that you choose in Settings. A copied secret
          stays on this iPhone and expires after 60 seconds by default.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On the iPhone only.</strong> For AutoFill, iOS keeps the username and the website host of each login
          on the iPhone, so it can suggest them. The camera is used only to scan the code that your Mac shows, and no
          picture is saved. When you move items to or from another app (Credential Exchange), iOS carries them between
          the two apps on this iPhone. "Open" on a website opens it in Safari. None of this goes to us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where your vault goes.</strong> You choose iCloud Drive, the Apassy relay, or neither. In iCloud
          Drive, Apple holds the encrypted vault file for you, under Apple's terms; we have no access to it. Through the
          relay, Apassy sends the encrypted vault file and gets the copies of your other devices. Nobody can read a copy
          without your passphrase, and the passphrase never leaves your devices.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Requests from your agents.</strong> When a coding agent on your Mac asks for a credential, your Mac
          sends the request to your iPhone and your Apple Watch through the Apassy relay. The request, your answer, and
          the list of agents of your Macs are sealed on your devices (ChaCha20-Poly1305) with a key that only your
          devices have. The relay passes them on and cannot read them. The older Wi-Fi pairing with a Mac talks to your
          Mac on your local network only, over TLS with a pinned certificate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Notifications.</strong> If you turn them on, iOS gives Apassy a push token. Apassy sends it to the
          Apassy relay, so the relay can wake your iPhone when a request waits. The relay gives the token to our push
          service (a Cloudflare Worker), which asks Apple Push Notification service to show "A question is waiting". The
          notification has no other content: no agent, no credential, no vault. Turn notifications off in Apassy or in
          iOS Settings; remove the iPhone on your Mac to delete the token at the relay.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Apple Watch.</strong> The watch app holds no passwords. It keeps a small encrypted database with the
          keys it needs to read and answer requests, and its own keys in the watch's Secure Enclave and keychain. To
          connect it, your iPhone passes your Mac's code to the watch directly (WatchConnectivity) and does not keep it.
          The watch then talks to the Apassy relay itself, over Wi-Fi or cellular. It registers no push token.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Apassy relay</h2>
      <p>
        The hosted Apassy relay runs at <code>apassy-relay.wyderka.cc</code>, behind Cloudflare. Its code is open source
        (<a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/crates/apassy-relay`}>crates/apassy-relay</a>). It cannot read your vault copies,
        requests, or answers. It can see their sizes and times, the ids of your devices, their names and public keys,
        and push tokens. For each vault that uses it, it keeps:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The encrypted vault copy</strong>, the current and the previous version, with its signed head,
          versions, sizes, and times: until you turn relay sync off for the vault.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sealed requests and answers</strong>: the sealed content until 10 minutes after the request ends; the
          record of the request (devices, times, state) until 24 hours after it was made.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The sealed agent lists of your Macs</strong>: until the Mac is removed, or 30 days after the last
          update.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your devices</strong>: the name of each device (for example "iPhone", the name that you type, or the
          name of your watch), its public keys, and the id that the relay gives it, to know which devices belong to the
          vault and to check their signatures: until you remove the device on a Mac.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Push tokens</strong> of iPhones with notifications on: until you remove the iPhone on a Mac. A token
          that Apple refuses is no longer used.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A log of events</strong> (device ids, versions, sizes, times; no item names and no content), for
          security and operation. It has no fixed end date today: it stays while the vault's relay team exists, and an
          encrypted archive of it stays when the relay deletes an unused team.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Backups</strong> of the relay's data (encrypted, as on the server), made each day and kept for 14 days on
          the server, with a copy off the server.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The IP address and time of each request</strong>, in the usual request logs of Cloudflare and of the
          server, to deliver and protect the service (for example rate limits).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We use this data only to run the relay. We do not sell it, share it for advertising, or use it to profile you.
        You can also run the relay on your own server: your Mac then links your iPhone and watch to that relay, and your
        vault copies, requests, and answers go to your server instead of ours.
      </p>

      <h2>The browser extension</h2>
      <p>
        The Apassy extension for Chromium browsers fills, saves, and creates website logins from the Apassy app on the same
        Mac. It also fills one-time codes and can answer passkey requests. It has no servers and makes no network requests.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What it reads.</strong> The address and selected login form of the active tab when you use the toolbar.
          Saving or updating a login explicitly reads the username and password from that form. If you enable login
          suggestions for a site, focusing a login field reads its site and username prefix to retrieve matching account
          titles and usernames from Apassy. Focus does not release or fill a password. Optional passkey support reads the
          site's passkey request.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where the data goes.</strong> Only to the Apassy app on the same Mac, through the browser's native
          messaging, and into the selected website form or back to the site that requested a passkey. Apassy asks for
          fresh Touch ID or passphrase approval before each fill, save, update, new login, one-time code, or passkey
          operation. Listing account metadata does not require this approval. The extension never sends the data to us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What it keeps.</strong> Nonsecret feature preferences are retained through browser site permissions and
          registered scripts. Extension-only browser session storage holds action results and recovery metadata: site
          origin, tab, document and form identifiers, time, and result status. Results are usable for up to five minutes
          and are removed when a later check finds them expired or their page/document target changed. This storage
          contains no passwords, passkeys, one-time codes, account labels, or page query strings. Passwords and codes are
          handled only for the requested operation, and the extension never uses the clipboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How the data is used.</strong> Only to suggest, fill, save, create, or update logins and to use one-time
          codes and passkeys from your Apassy vault. It is not
          sold, not given to third parties, not used for ads, and not used to decide credit or loans.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Permissions.</strong> <code>nativeMessaging</code> to talk to the Apassy app, <code>activeTab</code>
          to access the active page after you click the button, <code>scripting</code> to run the packaged scripts for
          these operations, and <code>storage</code> for the nonsecret session results described above. Site access to HTTPS
          sites and local test pages on <code>localhost</code> is optional and requires your choice and the browser's
          permission prompt. Login suggestions run only on enabled sites and support frames with the same origin as the
          top page. Toolbar actions, one-time codes, and passkeys target the top frame. Turning a feature off stops its
          scripts; revoke its site access in the browser's extension settings to remove the grant.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The site and its downloads are served by Cloudflare. It sets no cookies and has no analytics or trackers.</li>
        <li>
          Cloudflare keeps the usual request logs (IP address, time, address, browser) to run and protect the site. We use
          them only for that.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Open an issue on <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>GitHub</a>. Report a security problem privately,
        as <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>SECURITY.md</a> says. The code of the apps, the extension, and the
        relay is public, so you can check each point above.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Page>

<style>
  main { max-width: 820px; padding-top: 72px; padding-bottom: 120px; }
  .head { padding-bottom: 36px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  h1 { margin-top: 12px; font-size: clamp(40px, 6vw, 64px); font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.05em; line-height: 1.02; }
  .lead { margin-top: 16px; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 48px; font-size: 28px; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.03em; }
  .prose p { margin-top: 14px; color: var(--muted); }
  .prose ul { margin-top: 14px; padding-left: 0; list-style: none; display: grid; gap: 12px; }
  .prose li { position: relative; padding-left: 22px; color: var(--muted); }
  .prose li::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 4px; top: 0.7em; width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--blue); }
  .prose li strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .prose a { color: var(--blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--blue) 35%, transparent); text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .prose code { font-size: 0.88em; padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 5px; background: var(--surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--edge); color: var(--ink-2); }
</style>
